Assembler Version 15
====================
Assembled by: version 14 (both instgen and asm)

Features Supported by This Assembler
-------------------------------------
Same external features as version 14. Internal improvements to local label
storage and hash table performance:

Syntax:
- Non-standard (custom) mnemonic syntax: LDA#, LDAZ, STAZ, etc.
- DATA pseudo-instruction for raw byte emission
- Hex constants: $xx (1 byte) or $xxxx (2 bytes)
- Quoted strings with \n and \\ escapes
- Label definitions with PC value or explicit assignment (including label refs)
- PC setting via: * = $xxxx (with zero-fill on pass 2)
- Low byte (<) and high byte (>) operators on labels
- Relative branch labels (auto-detected via instruction flags)
- Labels as 2-byte operands
- Comments with semicolons
- Two-pass assembly
- .include directive
- .zeropage and .code section directives
- Command-line arguments: <assembler> <input> <output> [debug]
- Local labels with . prefix, scoped to enclosing global label

Directives:
- .include filename
- .zeropage - switches to zero page section
- .code - switches back to code section

Instruction set: same as version 14

Error handling: same as version 14 (errors 1-10)

New in this version (relative to version 14):
- Heap-based global label storage: CURR_GLOBAL_HEAP_L/H stores a pointer to
  the global label string on the heap, replacing the CURR_GLOBAL buffer at
  $1C00. This eliminates the need to copy the global label name.
- $01 escape format for local labels on the heap: local labels are stored as
  $01 <addr_lo> <addr_hi> ".local" $00, where the address is a pointer to
  the global label's heap entry. This allows scope verification during lookup
  by comparing the stored pointer against CURR_GLOBAL_HEAP.
- Cached hash optimization (CACHED_HASH): when a global label is defined,
  its pre-ASL hash value is cached. Local label hash calculation starts from
  this cached value rather than rehashing the global prefix, saving time.
- Separate hash functions: calculate_hash (global labels, updates hash),
  calculate_hash_local (starts from CACHED_HASH), and
  calculate_hash_instruction (global hash, does NOT modify CACHED_HASH)
- find_in_hash_instruction: separate lookup path for instruction opcodes that
  avoids modifying the cached hash state
- IS_LOCAL_LABEL flag: used throughout hash table code to select between
  global and local label handling
- compare_token handles both normal strings and $01 escape format, verifying
  scope pointer matches before comparing local label names
- Emit optimization: INC PC moved before PASS/IN_ZEROPAGE checks, with
  write as a tail call instead of inline JSR
- select_instruction_hash_table in common.asm clears IS_LOCAL_LABEL flag
- commit_cached_hash function: explicitly saves pre-ASL hash to CACHED_HASH
  when updating global label scope

Features Used in This Assembler's Source Code
----------------------------------------------
- Non-standard mnemonic syntax (LDA#, LDAZ, STAZ, BITZ, INCZ, ASLZ, etc.)
- DATA pseudo-instruction
- DATA $00 for zero page variable allocation in .zeropage section
- Hex constants ($xx and $xxxx)
- Quoted strings with escape sequences
- Labels and label assignments (label = value)
- PC setting (* = $2000 and * = $FFFC for vectors)
- Low byte (<) and high byte (>) operators
- Labels as absolute 2-byte operands
- Comments (;)
- BRK with inline error code and message
- .include directive (out/inst.asm.out, environment.asm, common.asm,
  file_stack.asm, to_decimal.asm)
- .zeropage and .code section directives
- Local labels (.label syntax) throughout the codebase
- Command-line arg access: argc, argv
- File I/O: open, close, read, openout, write

New syntax used in source (relative to version 14):
- Local labels with . prefix (.loop, .done, .no_file, .skip, etc.) - first
  version where the assembler's own source uses local labels

Other source changes:
- CURR_GLOBAL_HEAP_L/H replaces CURR_GLOBAL buffer ($1C00 no longer used)
- IS_LOCAL_LABEL and CACHED_HASH variables added to zero page
- LHASHTAB back at $1E00 (same as v14), TOKEN at $1D00
- hash_table.asm significantly more complex: escape format storage/comparison,
  three separate calculate_hash variants, find_in_hash split into
  find_in_hash and find_in_hash_instruction with shared find_in_hash_common
